# Experiment Design: TorchOpt Explicit-Gradient API vs. Standard Autograd
Status: DESIGN ONLY. No experiment has been executed. All API names for TorchOpt are descriptive and must be confirmed against the installed package version at implementation time.

## 1. Hypotheses
Primary outcome 1 — Computation time (T):
- H0_T: For matched model/size/dtype, TorchOpt's explicit single-pass gradient API has the same per-iteration forward+gradient time as native autograd (no-retention), i.e., median time ratio A/B = 1.
- H1_T: TorchOpt's explicit gradient API has lower per-iteration time than at least one native-autograd baseline, i.e., median ratio A/B < 1 or A/C < 1 (two-sided against equivalence margin; see Analysis).

Primary outcome 2 — Numerical stability (S), primary metric = gradient accuracy:
- H0_S: Under ill-conditioned inputs, the relative L2 error of TorchOpt gradients vs. a float64 ground truth is equal to that of native autograd (no-retention), i.e., median error ratio = 1.
- H1_S: TorchOpt gradients have lower relative L2 error (improvement) under ill-conditioning, i.e., error ratio A/B < 1 beyond the non-inferiority threshold.
Secondary (trajectory stability): over 200 fixed optimizer steps with 10 seeds, per-step and final loss (mean +/- std across seeds) are compared across arms; H1_S2 (exploratory): TorchOpt yields equal or lower cross-seed variance of final loss.

## 2. Experimental Arms
- Arm A (treatment): TorchOpt's single-pass forward+gradient call, which reuses intermediate activations to minimize allocation (exact API name/arguments to be confirmed against installed torchopt version X.Y at implementation; pin the version in the environment spec).
- Arm B (primary standard baseline): native PyTorch autograd, loss.backward() / torch.autograd.grad(create_graph=False), no graph retention, fresh graph per iteration.
- Arm C (memory-reuse counterfactual): native autograd with retain_graph=True so intermediates are reused across sequential gradient uses within an iteration — the standard-autograd cost of the same "reuse" behavior.
- Arm D (accuracy reference, timing-excluded): central finite differences (h relative to |param|) against a float64 implementation; used only for gradient-accuracy anchoring and convergence-order verification, on a small subset of configs (FD cost scales with parameter count).
- Arm E (ablation, if feasible): TorchOpt run in a no-reuse / recompute mode, if the API exposes one. If unavailable, drop Arm E and state the resulting confound in Limitations; Arm C serves as the closest standard-autograd counterfactual for the memory-reuse benefit.

## 3. Conditions and Sweeps
| Axis | Levels | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| Architecture | MLP (2 and 4 hidden layers, ReLU, linear head); small CNN (3 conv blocks, 32x32x3 input); LSTM (1 layer, hidden 128, seq len 16) | MLP gets full factorial; CNN/LSTM get reduced factorial |
| Input dim / params | 64 / 512 / 2048 (MLP); ~10^3 to ~10^6 params | Parameter-count axis to locate where reuse effects emerge |
| Batch size | 64 / 256 / 1024 | All architectures |
| Condition / input scale | Input magnitude scale 10^1 / 10^2 / 10^3 / 10^4 plus a whitened control (effective condition numbers ~10^2 to 10^8 in fp32) | Stability stress axis |
| dtype | fp32 (primary), fp64 (reference only; arms A-D in fp64 for the ground-truth gradients) | |
| Seeds | 10 fixed seeds for data/init; 3 timing seeds | Shared across arms |

FD subset (Arm D): MLP, input dim 64, batch 64, 1-2 condition levels; h in {1e-2, 1e-3, 1e-4} for convergence-order check.

## 4. Measurement Protocol
- Per arm, per config: build identical model + data (same seed), then:
  1. Warmup: 5 un-timed iterations (JIT/thread pools, allocator caches).
  2. Timed: 40 iterations (20 for the largest configs), each iteration = one forward + one gradient pass; record per-iteration wall time with time.perf_counter_ns.
  3. Memory: peak RSS over the timed block via resource.getrusage(RUSAGE_SELF).ru_maxrss (or psutil), recorded per arm; report delta vs. process baseline.
- Threads: torch.set_num_threads(1) and torch.set_num_interop_threads(1) for all timing runs (primary); a 4-thread condition is a secondary check. CPU affinity pinning (taskset) recommended.
- Randomization: arm execution order randomized per config (seeded permutation) to guard against thermal/drift bias.
- Seeds: fixed global seed list; same data tensors and init across arms within a config (arms are separate processes or fresh graphs to avoid state leakage).
- Stability measurement: for a subset of configs (all condition levels, dim 512, batch 256), compute one gradient per arm in fp32 and one in fp64; reference = fp64 Arm B gradient (and Arm D on the FD subset).
- Trajectory check (secondary): 200 steps of Adam (fixed lr per architecture), 10 seeds, record per-step loss; arms A, B, C only.

## 5. Analysis Plan
Time:
- Unit of analysis: per-iteration time, aggregated per config-arm to median and IQR.
- Primary test: paired comparison within config (A vs B, A vs C); overall fit a linear mixed-effects model on log(time): fixed effects arm and architecture, random intercept per config; report median ratio with 95% CI (bootstrap over configs).
- Pre-registered decision rule (time): "faster" = 95% CI of median ratio entirely below 0.90 (>=10% faster); "equivalent" = CI within [0.95, 1.05]; otherwise "no improvement / slower".
Stability:
- Primary: relative L2 gradient error (fp32 arm vs fp64 reference), per arm, as a function of condition number; fit log-log error curves and report slope per arm.
- Pre-registered decision rule (stability): "improvement" = median error ratio A/B <= 0.5; "equivalent" = within [0.5, 2.0] (non-inferiority margin 2x); "worse" = > 2.0 or any inf/nan where baselines are finite.
- FD check: central-difference residual vs analytic/fp64 gradient must show log-log slope in [1.8, 2.2] in h; if not, the FD reference is deemed invalid and fp64-native-autograd is the sole anchor (flagged).
- Secondary trajectory: compare mean final loss and cross-seed std across arms A/B/C; report effect sizes (Cohen's d on log loss); exploratory, not power-driven.
- Multiple comparisons: Holm correction across the two time contrasts and the stability contrast.

## 6. Controls and Confound Mitigation
- Identical model, data, seed, dtype, thread settings across arms; only the differentiation path differs.
- Randomized arm order per config; single-threaded timing; CPU affinity pinning; thermal break (sleep) between warm and cold config blocks if runtime allows.
- Memory-reuse isolation: Arm E ablation if available; otherwise Arm C (retain_graph) is the closest counterfactual and the residual confound (other TorchOpt optimizations) is stated as a limitation.
- Arm D is excluded from timing comparisons so FD cost does not pollute the time analysis.
- All arms use the same PyTorch version; TorchOpt version pinned and recorded in the report.

## 7. Feasibility Assessment
Required environment (sandbox currently lacks all of these; network disabled here):
- Python 3.10-3.12 (sandbox is 3.14; prefer 3.11 for torch wheel availability), pip with network access.
- torch==2.x CPU wheel (pinned), torchopt==<latest, pinned at implementation (record exact version), numpy, psutil, scipy, statsmodels (mixed models).
- CPU: 16 cores assumed; no GPU required.
Estimated cost:
- Per MLP config: ~45 x (fwd+bwd) iterations; at dim 2048/batch 1024 on 1 core, ~10-50 ms per iteration -> < 3 s per config; smaller configs < 0.5 s.
- Full factorial (MLP) ~ 3 depths x 3 dims x 3 batches x 5 conditions x 3 arms ~ 400 configs; CNN/LSTM reduced ~ 150 configs. Timing total ~ 30-60 min single-core, ~ 15-30 min on 16 cores.
- fp64 stability subset + fp32 subset: ~ 10-20 min. FD subset: bounded by parameter count (dim 64) ~ 10-30 min. Trajectory: 200 steps x 10 seeds x 3 arms x 3 archs ~ 1-3 h (dominant cost).
- Total: ~ 2-5 h wall on 16 cores.
If compute-constrained, drop in order: (1) LSTM architecture; (2) trajectory secondary check (or cut to 5 seeds, 100 steps); (3) largest dim/batch levels; (4) condition sweep to 3 levels; (5) 4-thread secondary condition.

## 8. Limitations
- Confound: without Arm E, any time/stability difference cannot be attributed solely to intermediate-data reuse; TorchOpt's other optimizations (kernels, graph handling) co-vary with the treatment. Arm C is the best available counterfactual.
- Generalizability: three CPU-friendly architectures are a bounded sample; conclusions about "TorchOpt vs standard autograd" may not extend to other architectures or to GPU workloads, where autograd memory retention is typically more consequential.
- CPU-only: no GPU means memory-allocation savings (the stated mechanism) are only observable via coarse RSS, and timing results reflect CPU execution; GPU transfer/allocator dynamics are out of scope.
- RSS is a coarse memory proxy (OS-level, not allocator-level); it captures large allocation differences but not peak transient allocations.
- TorchOpt API/version drift: descriptive API references must be verified at implementation; behavior may differ across versions.
- FD reference is only valid on the small subset; the fp64 native-autograd anchor inherits native-autograd's own (small) fp64 error.

NOTE: This is a design document. No experiment was executed; no empirical results are claimed.
