d_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-eb19eea6-fb5c-4d5a-8726-c514bd811a6b", "tool_call": {"id": "call-eb19eea6-fb5c-4d5a-8726-c514bd811a6b", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{"evidence": "Repository observations: /workspace contains only task.json (no TorchOpt source/docs/config); task.json documents track note confirms design-only (\"repositories and compute are not required\"). Environment checks: python3 = 3.14.4; `import torch` fails; pip list shows no torch/torchopt/numpy/scipy; network disabled; gpu_enabled=false; nproc=224 (design assumes a 16-core target per the researcher's spec). The design document was written to /workspace/EXPERIMENT_DESIGN.md (83 lines) and verified. No experiment was executed; all cost figures are estimates, not measurements.", "uncertainties": ["Whether TorchOpt exposes a no-reuse ablation mode (determines availability of Arm E).", "Exact TorchOpt version to pin and whether its API matches the descriptive references.", "Whether fp64 native-autograd is an acceptable sole anchor if FD validation fails.", "Wall-clock estimates for the trajectory check (largest uncertainty in the 2\u20135 h total).", "Whether the target machine is 16 cores as specified; estimates scale roughly linearly with core count for the parallelizable config sweep."], "summary": "Deliver full experiment design document", "stage": "result", "answer": "# Experiment Design: TorchOpt Explicit-Gradient API vs. Standard Autograd\n\nStatus: **DESIGN ONLY.** No experiment has been executed; no empirical results are claimed. All TorchOpt API references are descriptive and must be confirmed against the installed package version at implementation time. A copy is saved at `/workspace/EXPERIMENT_DESIGN.md`.\n\n## 1. Hypotheses\n**Primary outcome 1 \u2014 Computation time (T)**\n- H0_T: For matched model/size/dtype, TorchOpt's explicit single-pass gradient API has the same per-iteration forward+gradient time as native autograd (no-retention); median time ratio A/B = 1.\n- H1_T: TorchOpt's explicit gradient API has lower per-iteration time than at least one native-autograd baseline (median ratio A/B < 1 or A/C < 1), decided against pre-registered margins (Section 5).\n\n**Primary outcome 2 \u2014 Numerical stability (S); primary metric = gradient accuracy**\n- H0_S: Under ill-conditioned inputs, the relative L2 error of TorchOpt gradients vs. a float64 ground truth equals that of native autograd (no-retention); median error ratio = 1.\n- H1_S: TorchOpt gradients show lower relative L2 error under ill-conditioning beyond the non-inferiority threshold (error ratio A/B \u2264 0.5).\n\n**Secondary (trajectory stability, exploratory)**: over 200 fixed Adam steps with 10 seeds, per-step and final loss (mean \u00b1 std across seeds) are compared across arms A/B/C. H1_S2: TorchOpt yields equal or lower cross-seed variance of final loss.\n\n## 2. Experimental Arms\n- **Arm A (treatment):** TorchOpt's single-pass forward+gradient call, which reuses intermediate activations to minimize allocation. Exact API name/arguments *to be confirmed against installed torchopt version X.Y*; the version is pinned and recorded in the report.\n- **Arm B (primary standard baseline):** native PyTorch autograd \u2014 `loss.backward()` / `torch.autograd.grad(create_graph=False)`, no graph retention, fresh graph per iteration.\n- **Arm C (memory-reuse counterfactual):** native autograd with `retain_graph=True`, so intermediates are reused across sequential gradient uses within an iteration \u2014 the standard-autograd cost of the same \"reuse\" behavior.\n- **Arm D (accuracy reference, timing-excluded):** central finite differences (h relative to |param|) against a float64 implementation; used only for gradient-accuracy anchoring and convergence-order verification on a small config subset (FD cost scales with parameter count).\n- **Arm E (ablation, if feasible):** TorchOpt in a no-reuse/recompute mode, if the API exposes one. If unavailable, Arm E is dropped and the resulting confound is stated in Limitations; Arm C serves as the closest standard-autograd counterfactual for the memory-reuse benefit.\n\n## 3. Conditions and Sweeps\n| Axis | Levels | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Architecture | MLP (2 and 4 hidden layers, ReLU, linear head); small CNN (3 conv blocks, 32\u00d732\u00d73); LSTM (1 layer, hidden 128, seq len 16) | MLP: full factorial; CNN/LSTM: reduced factorial |\n| Input dim / params | 64 / 512 / 2048 (MLP); \u224810\u00b3\u201310\u2076 params overall | Parameter-count axis to locate where reuse effects emerge |\n| Batch size | 64 / 256 / 1024 | All architectures |\n| Condition / input scale | Input magnitude 10\u00b9/10\u00b2/10\u00b3/10\u2074 + whitened control (effective condition numbers \u224810\u00b2\u201310\u2078 in fp32) | Stability stress axis |\n| dtype | fp32 (primary); fp64 (reference: arms A\u2013D in fp64 produce ground-truth gradients) | |\n| Seeds | 10 fixed seeds (data/init); 3 timing seeds | Shared across arms within a config |\n\nArm D subset: MLP, dim 64, batch 64, 1\u20132 condition levels; h \u2208 {1e-2, 1e-3, 1e-4} for convergence-order verification.\n\n## 4. Measurement Protocol\nPer arm, per config (identical model + data, same seed):\n1. **Warmup:** 5 un-timed iterations (thread pools, allocator caches).\n2. **Timed:** 40 iterations (20 for the largest configs); each = one forward + one gradient pass; per-iteration wall time via `time.perf_counter_ns`.\n3. **Memory:** peak RSS over the timed block via `resource.getrusage(RUSAGE_SELF).ru_maxrss` (or psutil), per arm; report delta vs. process baseline.\n\n- **Threads:** `torch.set_num_threads(1)`, `torch.set_num_interop_threads(1)` for all timing (primary); 4-thread condition as secondary check; CPU affinity pinning (taskset) recommended.\n- **Randomization:** arm execution order randomized per config (seeded permutation) to guard against thermal/drift bias.\n- **Seeds:** fixed global seed list; same data tensors and init across arms; arms run in separate processes (or fresh graphs) to avoid state leakage.\n- **Stability measurement:** on the stability subset (all condition levels, dim 512, batch 256), one gradient per arm in fp32 and fp64; reference = fp64 Arm B gradient (plus Arm D on the FD subset).\n- **Trajectory (secondary):** 200 Adam steps (fixed lr per architecture), 10 seeds, per-step loss recorded; arms A/B/C only.\n\n## 5. Analysis Plan\n**Time**\n- Unit of analysis: per-iteration time, aggregated per config-arm to median + IQR.\n- Primary: paired within-config contrasts (A vs B, A vs C); overall linear mixed-effects model on log(time) with fixed arm and architecture effects and random intercept per config; report median ratio with 95% CI (bootstrap over configs).\n- Pre-registered decision rule: **\"faster\"** = 95% CI of median ratio entirely below 0.90 (\u226510% faster); **\"equivalent\"** = CI within [0.95, 1.05]; otherwise \"no improvement / slower\".\n\n**Stability**\n- Primary: relative L2 gradient error (fp32 arm vs fp64 reference) per arm as a function of condition number; fit log-log error curves and report slope per arm.\n- Pre-registered decision rule: **\"improvement\"** = median error ratio A/B \u2264 0.5; **\"equivalent\"** = within [0.5, 2.0] (non-inferiority margin 2\u00d7); **\"worse\"** = > 2.0 or any inf/nan where baselines are finite.\n- FD validation: central-difference residual vs fp64 gradient must show log-log slope in [1.8, 2.2] in h; if not, the FD reference is declared invalid and fp64 native-autograd is the sole anchor (flagged in report).\n- Secondary trajectory: mean final loss and cross-seed std across A/B/C; effect sizes (Cohen's d on log loss); exploratory, not power-driven.\n- Holm correction across the two time contrasts and the stability contrast.\n\n## 6. Controls and Confound Mitigation\n- Identical model, data, seed, dtype, thread settings across arms; only the differentiation path differs.\n- Randomized arm order per config; single-threaded timing; CPU affinity pinning; thermal break between warm/cold config blocks if runtime allows.\n- Memory-reuse isolation: Arm E ablation if available; otherwise Arm C (retain_graph) is the closest counterfactual and the residual confound (other TorchOpt optimizations) is stated as a limitation.\n- Arm D excluded from timing comparisons so FD cost does not pollute the time analysis.\n- Same PyTorch version across all arms; TorchOpt version pinned and recorded.\n\n## 7. Feasibility Assessment\n**Required environment** (this sandbox lacks all of these; network is disabled here):\n- Python 3.10\u20133.12 (sandbox has 3.14; prefer 3.11 for torch wheel availability), pip with network access.\n- Pinned torch==2.x CPU wheel, torchopt (pinned at implementation, exact version recorded), numpy, psutil, scipy, statsmodels.\n- 16-core CPU assumed; no GPU required.\n\n**Estimated cost**\n- Per MLP config: ~45 fwd+bwd iterations; at dim 2048/batch 1024 on 1 core \u224810\u201350 ms per iteration \u2192 <3 s per config; smaller configs <0.5 s.\n- Full MLP factorial \u2248 3 depths \u00d7 3 dims \u00d7 3 batches \u00d7 5 conditions \u00d7 3 arms \u2248 400 configs; CNN/LSTM reduced \u2248 150 configs. Timing total \u2248 30\u201360 min single-core, \u2248 15\u201330 min on 16 cores.\n- fp32/fp64 stability subset \u2248 10\u201320 min; FD subset (bounded by dim 64) \u2248 10\u201330 min; trajectory check \u2248 1\u20133 h (dominant cost).\n- **Total \u2248 2\u20135 h wall on 16 cores.**\n\n**If compute-constrained, drop in order:** (1) LSTM architecture; (2) trajectory secondary check (or cut to 5 seeds / 100 steps); (3) largest dim/batch levels; (4) condition sweep to 3 levels; (5) 4-thread secondary condition.\n\n## 8. Limitations\n- **Confound:** without Arm E, time/stability differences cannot be attributed solely to intermediate-data reuse; TorchOpt's other optimizations (kernels, graph handling) co-vary with the treatment. Arm C is the best available counterfactual.\n- **Generalizability:** three CPU-friendly architectures are a bounded sample; conclusions may not extend to other architectures or GPU workloads, where autograd memory retention is typically more consequential.\n- **CPU-only:** memory-allocation savings (the stated mechanism) are observable only via coarse RSS; timing results reflect CPU execution; GPU allocator/transfer dynamics are out of scope.\n- **RSS is a coarse memory proxy** (OS-level, not allocator-level): captures large allocation differences, not peak transient allocations.\n- **TorchOpt API/version drift:** descriptive API references must be verified at implementation; behavior may differ across versions.\n- **FD reference validity** is only established on the small subset; the fp64 native-autograd anchor inherits native autograd's own (small) fp64 error.", "assumptions": ["TorchOpt's explicit gradient API is a single-pass forward+gradient call with intermediate reuse; exact names pinned at implementation against the installed version.", "Two-baseline treatment of 'standard autograd' (no-retention + retain_graph) per the approved approach.", "Arm E (no-reuse ablation) may be unavailable; design degrades gracefully to Arm C as counterfactual with stated confound.", "Execution will occur in a network-enabled environment with pinned CPU torch + torchopt on ~16 cores; this sandbox cannot run it.", "Pre-registered margins (time: 0.90/0.95\u20131.05; stability: 0.5/2.0) are defensible defaults the researcher may re-tune before implementation."]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-f272a48a50bd4b8aa4086134b745ba87", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": "Deliver full experiment design document", "kind": "ActionEvent"}
